                <pb facs="0460" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v1/pg446"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Monroe, Secretary
                        of State, to Governor Claiborne.</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of
                        State</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date calendar="gregorian" when="1811-12-05"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">December</hi> 5, 1811</date>.</dateline>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit to you a copy
                    of a letter from Mr. Onis, from which it would appear that he had received
                    information *that some Frenchmen at New Orleans had it in view to arm and equip
                    at that place a vessel, for the purpose “of surprising the port of Baracoa, in
                    the island of Cuba, plundering its inhabitants, and laying waste the town.” <seg
                        type="margin">[4]</seg></p>
                <p>Such an equipment in the port of New Orleans would be highly improper, and the
                    President does not doubt that you will take effectual measures to prevent it,
                    if, on inquiry, you find there is any foundation for the report which has
                    reached Mr. Onis.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>JAMES MONROE.</item>
                            <item>Governor <hi rendition="#small-caps">Claiborne.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
